{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"New evidence: the sawtooth's AMPLITUDE scales with merge burst rate, and I have now watched it do so.\n\n**Measured 10:29-10:34 UTC.** `root_disk_used_percent`, 5-minute Maximum: `69 70 69 69 70 77 72`. The excursion to **77% is the highest reading of the night** — roughly +8 points, about 5 GB, against the ~2 GB / ~3.3-point sawtooth the diagnosis characterised earlier.\n\n**What preceded it.** TWO changes merged in quick succession immediately before: 2672 (another session's) and 2665 (mine, submitted at ~10:31 UTC). Each merge advances `main`, and `rebar-autodeploy.timer` (`OnUnitActiveSec=2min`) rebuilds on every advance, with container recreates measured at ~21 minutes during which old and new writable layers coexist.\n\nSo two merges inside a few minutes means two rebuild cycles whose layer-coexistence windows OVERLAP, and the excursions stack rather than alternate. That is a straightforward consequence of the mechanism already established on this ticket, but it is worth recording as a measurement because it converts the diagnosis into an operational lever.\n\n**The lever, and I am applying it to myself.** My own submit cadence is an input to this. Over the preceding ninety minutes I merged 2669, 2670, 2671 and 2665, and `main` advanced seven times in the window the diagnosis covered. Spacing submits so a rebuild completes before the next one starts should keep the excursions from stacking. I have adjusted accordingly — the remaining changes in my queue will go one at a time, with root allowed to retreat between them, rather than as a burst whenever votes happen to land together.\n\n**Stated as correlation, with the caveat.** This is one observation of a two-merge burst producing a larger-than-usual excursion. I did not instrument the autodeploy runs themselves, so \"two merges\" and \"two rebuilds\" is inference from the timer's documented behaviour rather than from observed build starts. The discriminating measurement remains the one already named on this ticket: correlate excursion onsets against autodeploy START timestamps, not merge timestamps.\n\n**Headroom, so this is not read as alarming.** 77% is still below the 85% alarm, the excursion retreated to 72% within five minutes, and `disk_pressure_persists` has stayed 0 throughout. The sawtooth remains self-reclaiming. What has changed is only that its peaks are higher when merges cluster — which matters because the alarm is a threshold on the peak, not on the trend."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788604331872140871,"uuid":"5810a3b3-5902-426b-8c97-b2b7bae9aed4"}